ion of Theorem 1 consists of Sections 3 to 6;\\nAlso, the sequence of v-palindromes n (whether n < r(n) or not) is A338039 in the\\nOEIS [1]. After extensive computer calculation it is conjectured that there are no\\nprime v-palindromes [4, (a)]. In this paper, we attend to this conjecture and prove\\nthe following characterization of prime v-palindromes.\\nTheorem 1. The prime v-palindromes are precisely the primes of the form\\n5 · 10m −1 = 4 9 · · · 9\\n| {z }\\nm\\n,\\n(9)\\nfor some integer m ≥4, such that\\n5 · 10m −3 = 4 9 · · · 9\\n| {z }\\nm−1\\n7\\n(10)\\nis also prime.\\nIn Section 2, we prove various lemmas to be used throughout the rest of the\\npaper. The proof of the forward direction of Theorem 1 consists of Sections 3 to 6;\\nwhereas the proof of the converse consists of just Section 7.\\n2. Lemmas\\nWe start with some useful definitions.\\nDefinition 5. Let P denote any mathematical statement. Then the Iverson bracket\\nis defined by\\n[P] :=\\n(\\n1,\\nif P is true,\\n0,\\nif P is false.\\n(11)\\nDefinition 6. For integers α ≥1, denote ι(α) := α[α > 1]. That is,\\nι(α) :=\\n(\\n0,\\nif α = 1,\\nα,\\nif α > 1.\\n(12)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nv-PALINDROMES: AN ANALOGY TO THE PALINDROMES (https://web.williams.edu/Mathematics/sjmiller/public_html/math/papers/v_palindromes_%20AnalogyToPalindromes_Polymath2022v01.pdf)\\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; As mentioned in Subsection 1.1, the prime v-palindromes in base ... disprove Conjecture 23. ... The proof in the previous section is based on the equality v(5) =\\nv-PALINDROMES: AN ANALOGY TO THE PALINDROMES\\n19\\nAs mentioned in Subsection 1.1, the prime v-palindromes in base\\nten are characterized in [2]. This result, however, does not prove nor\\ndisprove Conjecture 23. Also noted in Subsection 1.1 is that there are\\nprime v-palindromes in bases 16 and 276. Hence we may consider\\nthe following problem.\\nProblem 1. Let b ≥2 be an integer. When does there exist a prime\\nv-palindrome in base b?\\nWe may also consider Conjectures 24 and 25 for a general base.\\n6.2. Two problems. While [16] provides an exact formula for the\\nnumber of palindromes up to a given positive integer, the same can\\nbe considered for v-palindromes, namely the following.\\nProblem 2. Let b ≥2 be an integer. Is there a formula for the number\\nof v-palindromes in base b up to a given positive integer? If not, how\\ncan it be approximated?\\nFrom 199 until 575 are 377 consecutive positive integers which\\nare not v-palindromes in base ten. Just as sequences of consecutive\\ncomposite numbers can be arbitrarily long, we may consider the\\nfollowing problem.\\nProblem 3. Let b ≥2 be an integer. Can a sequence of consecutive\\npositive integers each not a v-palindrome in base b be arbitrarily\\nlong?\\n6.3. Existence of v-palindromes in an arbitrary base. Section 4\\nshowed that v-palindromes exist in bases p + 1 and p2 + 1 for any\\nodd prime p.\\nSection 5 showed that v-palindromes exist in all\\nbasesb ≡120 (mod 330). However we are still left with the problem of\\ndetermining, for an arbitrary integer b ≥2, whether a v-palindrome\\nin base b exists.\\nThe proof in the previous section is based on the equality v(5) =\\nv(6). It is conceivable that the same method basing on other common\\nvalues of v will find other bases b for which a v-palindrome exists.\\nFor instance, we have\\nv(5) = v(6) = v(8) = v(9),\\nv(7) = v(10) = v(12) = v(18).\\nWe give the following table of the smallest v-palindrome, i.e.,\\nmin(Vb), for the first few bases, calculated using PARI/GP [14].--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA characterization of prime $v$-palindromes (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/372075048_A_characterization_of_prime_v-palindromes)\\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; We establish a characterization of prime v-palindromes: they are precisely the larger of twin prime pairs of the form $(5 \\\\cdot 10^m - 3, 5 \\\\cdot 10^m - 1)$, and thus standard conjectures on the distribution of twin primes imply that there are only finitely many prime v-palindromes. ... ble proof of Conjecture 1 by proving the following characterization of prime v- ... Therefore assume that p is a prime v-palindrome of m+ 1\\nThis means that 198 and 891 are v-palindromes. We establish a characterization of prime v-palindromes: they are precisely the larger of twin prime pairs of the form $(5 \\\\cdot 10^m - 3, 5 \\\\cdot 10^m - 1)$, and thus standard conjectures on the distribution of twin primes imply that there are only finitely many prime v-palindromes.\\n\\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\\n\\nDiscover the world's research\\n\\n  * 25+ million members\\n  * 160+ million publication pages\\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\\nJoin for free\\n\\nPublic Full-text 1\\n\\nContent uploaded by Muhammet Boran\\n\\nAuthor content\\n\\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Muhammet Boran on Oct 02, 2024\\n\\nContent may be subject to copyright.\\n\\narXiv:2307.00770v1 [math.NT] 3 Jul 2023\\n\\nA CHARACTERIZATION OF PRIME v-PALINDROMES\\n\\nMUHAMMET BORAN, GARAM CHOI, STEVEN J. MILLER, JESSE PURICE,\\n\\nAND DANIEL TSAI\\n\\nAbstract. An integer n≥1 is a v-palindrome if it is not a multiple of 10,\\n\\nnor a decimal palindrome, and such that the sum of the prime factors and\\n\\ncorresponding exponents larger than 1 in the prime factorization of n is equal\\n\\nto that of the integer formed by reversing the decimal digits of n. For example,\\n\\nif we take 198 and its reversal 891, their prime factorizations are 198 = 2·3 2·11\\n\\nand 891 = 3 4·11 respectively, and summing the numbers appearing in each\\n\\nfactorization both give 18. This means that 198 and 891 are v-palindromes.\\n\\nWe establish a characterization of prime v-palindromes: they are precisely the\\n\\nlarger of twin prime pairs of the form (5 ·10 m−3,5·10 m−1), and thus\\n\\nstandard conjectures on the distribution of twin primes imply that there are\\n\\nonly ﬁnitely many prime v-palindromes.\\n\\n1. Introduction\\n\\n1.1. Related Work. There have been many papers studying properties shared by\\n\\nnumbers and their reversals. We ﬁrst set some notation.\\n\\nDeﬁnition 1.1. Let b≥2, L≥1, and 0 ≤a 0, a 1,...,a L−1< b be any integers.\\n\\nIn\\n\\nparticular, there are inﬁnitely many v-palindromes. According to [Tsai0], the v-\\n\\npalindromes n≤10 5 with n < r(n) are\\n\\n18,198,576,819,1131,1304,1818,1998,2262,3393,4154,4636,8749,12441,\\n\\n14269,14344,15167,15602,16237,18018,18449,18977,19998,23843,24882,\\n\\n26677,26892,27225,29925,31229,36679,38967,39169,42788,45694,46215,\\n\\n46655,47259,48048,52416,56056,60147,62218,66218,79689,97999.\\n\\nThe sequence of v-palindromes n(whether n < r(n) or not) is A338039 in the\\n\\nOEIS [OEIS]. In [Tsai0], it is said that extensive computer calculations suggest the\\n\\nfollowing.\\n\\nConjecture 1. There are no prime v-palindromes.\\n\\n1.3. New Results. We are able to make signiﬁcant progress towards a possi-\\n\\nble proof of Conjecture 1 by proving the following characterization of prime v-\\n\\npalindromes.\\n\\nTheorem 1.1. The prime v-palindromes are precisely the primes of the form\\n\\n5·10 m−1 = 4 9 ···9\\n\\n|{z}\\n\\nm\\n\\n,(1.16)\\n\\nfor some integer m≥4, such that\\n\\n5·10 m−3 = 4 9 ···9\\n\\n|{z}\\n\\nm−1\\n\\n7 (1.17)\\n\\nis also prime.\\n\\nHere, the m≥4 is not very signiﬁcant and only means that it has been checked\\n\\nthat there are no prime v-palindromes of fewer than 5 decimal digits, and thus\\n\\nconceivably can be improved with more checking for small values of m. From this\\n\\ncharacterization of prime v-palindromes, it is a consequence of standard models for\\n\\nprimes (such as the Cram´er model, though weaker assumptions suﬃce) that there\\n\\nare only ﬁnitely many prime v-palindromes. In particular, we have the following.\\n\\n\\nv(r(p)) = v(2(p−2)) = 2 + (p−2) = p. (7.2)\\n\\nThis completes the proof.\\n\\n8. Number Of Prime v-palindromes\\n\\nTheorem 1.2 follows from standard models for prime numbers; we sketch below\\n\\nhow a slightly weakened Cram´er model, combined with our characterization of the\\n\\nform of prime v-palindromes, implies that there can only be ﬁnitely many.\\n\\nFor the standard Cram´er model, one assumes that each integer n is prime with\\n\\nprobability on the order of 1/log n, and the probability any two numbers are both\\n\\nprime is simply the product of the probabilities. This of course is clearly false, as we\\n\\nknow if n≥2 is even then it cannot be prime, and if n≡ −2 mod p for any prime\\n\\np < n then n+ 2 cannot be prime. However, our goal is simply to provide support,\\n\\nand thus we ignore the more reﬁned arguments one can do (see for example [Rub]).\\n\\nWe assume instead that the probability n and n+ 2 are both prime is bounded by\\n\\nC/ log 2 n for some ﬁxed C; as we are only trying to prove there are at most ﬁnitely\\n\\nmany prime v-palindromes, we are ﬁne with a slightly larger but still ﬁnite upper\\n\\nbound.\\n\\nLet T n be the event that 5 ·10 n−3 and 5 ·10 n−1 are both prime, then the\\n\\nexpected number of prime v-palindromes at most 10 N+1 is\\n\\nN\\n\\nX\\n\\nn=1\\n\\n1·Prob(T n).(8.1)\\n\\nAs we are just concerned with supporting the conjecture that there are only ﬁnitely\\n\\nmany, let us over-estimate and say\\n\\nProb(T n)≤C\\n\\nlog 2(5 ·10 n−3) ≤400 C\\n\\nn 2 log 2 10 ≤100 C\\n\\nn 2.(8.2)\\n\\nAs the sum of 1/n 2 converges, the expected number of prime v-palindromes is ﬁnite.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFull-Support Solutions and Rigidity for Three-Dimensional Evolution Algebras (https://www.preprints.org/manuscript/202610.1039)\\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; We disprove the conjecture of Camacho, Khudoyberdiyev and Omirov that \\\\(x_i^2 = \\\\sum_j b_{ij}x_j\\\\) has a solution with every coordinate nonzero for every nonsingular complex matrix \\\\(B\\\\). ... Camacho, Khudoyberdiyev and Omirov asked whether, for every nonsingular complex matrix $B=\\\\left({b}_{ij}\\\\right)$, the quadratic system ${x}_{i}^{2}={\\\\sum }_{j}{b}_{ij}{x}_{j}$ has a solution with all coordinates nonzero ([3] Conjecture 5.1). ... They also proved two conjectures concerning complete evolution algebras ([4] Theorems 3.2 and 3.3) and showed that every complex regular evolution algebra has a nonzero idempotent ([4] Theorem 3.5). ... Let E be an n-dimensional evolution algebra with natural basis ${e}_{1},\\\\dots ,{e}_{n}$ and invertible structure matrix $M=\\\\left({m}_{ij}\\\\right)$, where ${e}_{i}^{2}={\\\\sum }_{j}{m}_{ij}{e}_{j}$.\\n\\nVersion 2\\n\\nSubmitted:\\n\\n14 October 2026\\n\\nPosted:\\n\\n15 October 2026\\n\\nYou are already at the latest version\\n\\nPreprint\\n\\nArticle\\n\\nThis version is not peer-reviewed.\\n\\nFull-Support Solutions and Rigidity for Three-Dimensional Evolution Algebras\\n\\nXing-Yu Hu^{  *}\\n\\nXing-Yu Hu^{  *}\\n\\nVersion 2\\n\\nSubmitted:\\n\\n14 October 2026\\n\\nPosted:\\n\\n15 October 2026\\n\\nYou are already at the latest version\\n\\nAbstract\\n\\nWe disprove the conjecture of Camacho, Khudoyberdiyev and Omirov that \\\\(x_i^2 = \\\\sum_j b_{ij}x_j\\\\) has a solution with every coordinate nonzero for every nonsingular complex matrix \\\\(B\\\\). Dimension three is the smallest in which this conjecture fails. In that dimension, all counterexamples are equivalent under simultaneous coordinate permutations and nonzero diagonal rescalings. The exceptional locus in \\\\(\\\\mathrm{GL}_3(\\\\mathbb{C})\\\\) is the disjoint union of six smooth, closed, three-dimensional algebraic tori, each a complete intersection of codimension six. Every exceptional matrix has exactly seven nonzero entries, the minimum possible for a counterexample. A regular three-dimensional evolution algebra with at most six nonzero structure constants has a full-support idempotent, and this bound is sharp. We also give polynomial equations characterizing the exceptional locus. The proof uses elimination, traces in an Artinian complete intersection, and local multiplicities.\\n\\n###### Keywords: \\n\\nevolution algebras\\n\\n;  \\n\\nfull-support idempotents\\n\\n;  \\n\\nmonomial equivalence\\n\\n;  \\n\\nArtinian traces\\n\\n;  \\n\\nlocal multiplicities\\n\\n###### \\n\\nSubject: \\n\\nComputer Science and Mathematics  -   Algebra and Number Theory\\n\\nMSC:  Primary 17D92; Secondary 14M10\\n\\n## 1. Introduction\\n\\nEvolution algebras arose in the study of non-Mendelian inheritance [1]. Tian’s monograph [2] gives a detailed account of their theory. Camacho, Khudoyberdiyev and Omirov asked whether, for every nonsingular complex matrix $B=\\\\left({b}_{ij}\\\\right)$, the quadratic system ${x}_{i}^{2}={\\\\sum }_{j}{b}_{ij}{x}_{j}$ has a solution with all coordinates nonzero ([3] Conjecture 5.1). They verified the assertion in dimensions one and two. García-Martínez and Pérez-Rodríguez proved that, for $n\\\\ge 2$, a solution with at least two nonzero coordinates always exists ([4] Theorem 2.1). They also proved two conjectures concerning complete evolution algebras ([4] Theorems 3.2 and 3.3) and showed that every complex regular evolution algebra has a nonzero idempotent ([4] Theorem 3.5). Neither the two-coordinate existence result nor the nonzero-idempotent result guarantees full support in dimension three.\\n\\nWe exhibit a counterexample in dimension three and classify all nonsingular matrices for which full-support solvability fails. Theorem 4 shows that these matrices form a single class under the coordinate changes preserving the system. Corollary 2 describes their locus and gives the sharp lower bound of seven nonzero entries for a counterexample. Proposition 1 gives a polynomial criterion that does not require solving the quadratic system. Corollary 3 extends the sharp sparsity bound to structure matrices and shows that every simple three-dimensional complex evolution algebra has a full-support idempotent. The counterexample extends to higher dimensions by direct sum, but the classification here is restricted to dimension three.\\n\\nLet E be an n-dimensional evolution algebra with natural basis ${e}_{1},\\\\dots ,{e}_{n}$ and invertible structure matrix $M=\\\\left({m}_{ij}\\\\right)$, where ${e}_{i}^{2}={\\\\sum }_{j}{m}_{ij}{e}_{j}$. Such an E satisfies ${E}^{2}=E$, so it is regular (or perfect) ([9] Definition 2.3). For $x={({x}_{1},\\\\dots ,{x}_{n})}^{t}$, write ${x}^{\\\\left[2\\\\right]}={({x}_{1}^{2},\\\\dots ,{x}_{n}^{2})}^{t}$. A vector ${\\\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}$ is idempotent if and only if ${M}^{t}{x}^{\\\\left[2\\\\right]}=x$, or equivalently ${x}^{\\\\left[2\\\\right]}={\\\\left({M}^{t}\\\\right)}^{-1}x$. Such an idempotent has full support exactly when all its natural-basis coordinates are nonzero. For $n\\\\ge 2$, a one-dimensional subspace of E is a subalgebra if and only if it is spanned by a nonzero solution of this system ([5] Lemma 1). The case $n=1$ is immediate, and each such subalgebra contains a unique nonzero idempotent. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFull nonzero-coordinate solution to the nonlinear system x_i^2 = Σ_j a_{ij} x_j (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/cko19-conjecture-5-1-all-nonzero-solution)\\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Camacho, Khudoyberdiyev, and Omirov formulated a conjecture asserting the existence of a solution where all coordinates are nonzero for any complex invertible matrix A.\\n\\nThis note proves a relaxed version guaranteeing a nontrivial solution with at least two nonzero coordinates, sufficient for their applications to evolution algebras, but does not resolve the original full nonzero-coordinate conjecture.\\n\\n# Full nonzero-coordinate s